  "notes": "Guitar-driven country-pop with rock and, later, outright pop production — the entry's defining move is that \"banjo and ganjo\" are used \"as *rhythm* textures rather than bluegrass signifiers.\" That is authored as a drum fact, because it is one: \"the signature is a 16th-note-driven groove — closed hats or shaker in 16ths, kick on 1 and 'and-of-3,' snare hard on 2&4, with the ganjo/banjo playing continuous 16ths as a percussion layer.\" So the hat base is 16th, the perc density is the highest in this batch, and the kick takes a 3& secondary anchor. The melody is the entry's other distinctive claim: \"high tenor, syncopated, sixteenth-note phrasing that lands off the downbeat — the most rhythmically displaced vocal writing in this section,\" so `straightEighthsProb` is pushed down and the density up. Harmony is \"I–V–vi–IV and I–♭VII–IV,\" with the documented Knopfler/Buckingham influence showing \"as fingerstyle-derived arpeggiated harmony rather than block chords\" — the counter's arp weighting. The mixer's stated philosophy (\"I like to record performances; I don't like to record microphones,\" with a rough mix good enough to become the master) is why the humanize is a captured-take reading rather than a grid one. ⚠ A synth sub is described only as reinforcement \"layered under the electric bass\" on the pop-leaning records, so no `bass808` block is authored and the model keeps the genre's inherited live bass. ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: high-medium); the per-track readings are [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the perc density, the humanize values and the counter ratio.",
